  {system: "NVIDIA NeMo Curator semantic dedup / SemDeDup", url: "https://docs.nvidia.com/nemo-framework/user-guide/24.09/datacuration/semdedup.html", overlap: "Implements embedding clustering plus within-cluster pairwise similarity and duplicate removal; overlaps with the mitigation family, but not an explicit Jaccard-CC chain-overdelete diagnosis."},
  {system: "vLLM / SGLang / LMCache / FlashInfer / TensorRT-LLM", url: "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm ; https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang ; https://github.com/LMCache/LMCache", overlap: "Inference/KV-cache/kernel/runtime systems; no material overlap with training-corpus MinHash/CC dedup found from targeted search."}
]
CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: insight
SCALE_REALISM: The graph mechanism itself holds: the packet reports exact-edge CC innocent-removal 0.724 +/- 0.003 at T=0.8 moderate density, versus 0.000 for non-transitive and complete-linkage arms. But realistic workload magnitude is insufficient evidence: the packet admits the effect collapses to 0 at T=0.9 and depends on real-corpus drift-chain prevalence at T about 0.8. Existing shipping systems do run the vulnerable CC pattern at scale, but this artifact is synthetic L0 and does not yet measure C4/RedPajama/Stack component topology or downstream training harm.
RATIONALE: This is not integration-only glue because the contribution is a falsifiable diagnosis of a graph-topology failure mode in already-shipping MinHash/CC dedup systems, plus a mitigation comparison using the same edge set. It is also not green: BigCode/datatrove and NeMo already ship the core connected-components mechanism, SemDeDup-style workflows already ship a non-transitive-ish alternative, and the packet has not established real-corpus chain prevalence or model-quality cost. Reproducibility looks acceptable for L0 because it is CPU-only/stdlib/serial with 5 seeds and exact-edge controls, but systems acceptance needs an L1 run on a real corpus slice using the actual Google/Datatrove/NeMo pipeline and reporting component topology, innocent-removal fraction, wall-clock/resource costs, and downstream loss/diversity impact.
